statione militum, quae tunc excubabat: (4) metaph. of station or position;
Claud. 38, 3, pervenire ad susceptam stationem (= susceptum munus
imperii).

§ 2. ceterum: like the Gr. δ' οὖν, marking the return from a digression;
cf. Liv. xxxviii 55, ceterum ad hunc nimis aequum aut iniquum praetorem
reus extemplo factus L. Scipio.

reprehendisset: this reading is established against the variant deprehendisset by Calig. 45, veluti profugos ac reprehensos in catenis reduxit;
cf. Phaedr. v 8, 3, elapsum semel non ipse possit Iuppiter reprendere:
Plaut. Most. 863, servi fugiunt, sed si reprensi sunt, etc.

olim: in Latin, as in Greek, adverbs are often used like adjectives;
see the examples given by Kühner-Stegmann, i 218 sq.: Nägelsbach, Lat.
Stilistik § 75.

perseveraverunt: sc. ire (= iter continuaverunt), a rare elliptical use,
for which cf. Caes. B.C. iii 14, 2, una ex his (navibus), quae perseveravit..,
delata Oricum atque a Bibulo expugnata est.

Neronem: [Aur. Vict.] Epit. 10, 5, Iurgia autem sub patre venumdata;
rapinarum cupidum; unde Neronem cuncti opinantes vocantesque summam
rerum nactum graviter acceperant.

spectare supersedit: the use of the inf. with supersedeo (= abstineo)
is first found in the historian Sisenna (ob. 67 B.c.); not in Caes. or Cic.;
frequent in Livy and later writers.

§ 3. suscepta correctione morum: so Land some of the later MSS.;
the others have correptione, a word very often used by Christian writers.
For correctio morum (= censura) cf. Tib. 42, 1, in ipsa publicorum morum

fratris filiam: Julia, daughter of Titus and Marcia Furnilla (Tit. 4, 2);
she was married to Flavius Sabinus, a cousin of Domitian, who was put to
death by the emperor (Dom. 10, 4).
